{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391579": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113823 The role: The School of Psychology and Neuroscience is looking to recruit a full-time Administrative Assistant, until 31st March 2027, to provide a professional, efficient and friendly service. You will form part of a happy, dynamic executive support team who work closely together to ensure key School activities are managed and delivered on time. You will provide general office and clerical support in the newly formed School of Psychology and Neuroscience, performing a clearly defined range of standard tasks within established routines and procedures. This is a fixed-term contract until 31st March 2027 What will you be doing?: The Administrative Assistant acts as the first point of contact for most staff from across the University, external contacts and visitors. It is very important to ensure that those visiting the School are made to feel welcome and that their queries have been addressed to a high standard by exhibiting tact, discretion, maintaining confidentiality as required and demonstrating a steady hand regardless of the volume of work. Independence and initiative will be required to react to changing priorities and work circumstances, with scope to make decisions within very clear parameters. The role holder will organise their own day-to-day work to meet clear objectives providing an effective service to others. You should apply if: You should possess a high level of accuracy and flexibility, as well as excellent organisational and customer service skills, as these are all essential attributes for the role. You should be a good team player and also have excellent communication skills as you will be dealing with a wide range of colleagues on a daily basis. In addition, you should be experienced in using a range of IT systems and be able to adapt to new systems and processes quickly. Due to the fixed-term nature of the role, we would particularly welcome applications from candidates who, based on their experience and attitude, can demonstrate that they will hit the ground running in this role. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term until 31/03/2027 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: D Salary: £24,685 - £26,093 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience Shift pattern: 35 hours per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 19th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Isaac Bale - i.bale@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391415": {
      "title": "AI Infrastructure Associate Engineer (3 x FTE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/01/2027 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113121 The role: The Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) runs the Isambard-AI National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource, recently announced AI Data facility and the Isambard 3 Tier-2 Supercomputer. Isambard-AI is the most powerful supercomputer in the UK and amongst the most powerful in Europe. The BriCS team owns the entire process of developing and operating the centre’s compute and software infrastructure, which includes: The sourcing of hardware and system design. The deployment of huge software-defined infrastructure using tools such as Kubernetes and Terraform / OpenTofu. Building and operating platforms to enable researchers to conduct leading-edge research using the systems. Optimising and refining software to ensure environmental and economic efficient use. As one of the largest Open AI Research Resources internationally, we are committed to catalysing an AI transformation in the research and development community. In this role, you will work as part of the BriCS team to build and operate primarily the infrastructure and compute platforms that researchers use for their work. You do not need to be an AI or computational research domain expert to deliver world-class infrastructure, but you do need to quickly obtain a deep technical understanding of new domains. You should enjoy being self-directed and identifying the most important problems to solve as the team matures with standardized tools and processes around stability, robust service delivery and scaling. What will you be doing?: Use tools such as Python, Rust, Terraform / OpenTofu, Kubernetes, Git and Bash. Design and operate large, highly available supercomputing services managed as software-defined infrastructures, and integrated as complete computational experiments. You will experience designing and operating massive-scale GPU and combined CPU/GPU workloads across these services. You will design and debug platforms, and will work closely with researchers as you co-design solutions that will enable the development and operation of new algorithms and software to solve leading-edge research problems. You should apply if: Want to help build, maintain and secure some of the largest, modern software-defined supercomputing systems. Would enjoy working with world class domain and AI researchers as your primary workload . Have supported development or operation of a small to large clusters or dabbled in building your own physical or software-defined systems. Love operating large distributed, highly available systems, and want to see them used for truly open national-scale research in a cybersecurity compliant manner. For our AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Associate Engineer role focusing on storage and networking, you’ll need: Domain knowledge in 1 or more areas from SysOps, NetOps, DevOps, SecOps, MLOps or Research Software Engineering. Degree (or equivalent practical experience) in computer science, computational or ML/AI research or in a natural science with a high degree of competence in computer science or computational research. Ability to contribute as a member of diverse, technical teams and to follow operational procedures. The available job description provides a full view of the person specification. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ For any informal enquiries, please contact Emma Rose, Centre Manager - emma.rose@bristol.ac.uk. Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until August 2030. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Positions available: x3 FTE Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum. School/Unit: Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 31st January 2027 Please note that applications will be reviewed and shortlisted on a rolling basis throughout the advertising period. Interviews will also be held at regular intervals during this time. We reserve the right to close the advert early once the vacancies have been filled, therefore early applications are encouraged. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391478": {
      "title": "AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Engineer (3 x FTE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/01/2027 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113120 The role: The Bristol AI Supercomputing Centre runs the Isambard-AI National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource, recently announced AI Data facility and the Isambard3 Tier-2 Supercomputer. Isambard-AI is the most powerful supercomputer in the UK and amongst the most powerful in Europe. The AI Supercomputing team owns the entire process of developing and operating the centre’s compute and software infrastructure, which includes: The sourcing of hardware and system design. The deployment of huge software-defined infrastructure using tools such as Kubernetes and Terraform / OpenTofu. Building and operating platforms to enable researchers to conduct leading-edge research using the systems. Optimising and refining software to ensure environmental and economic efficient use. As one of the largest Open AI Research Resources internationally, we are committed to catalysing an AI transformation in the research and development community. In this role, you will work as part of the AI Supercomputing Team to build and operate primarily the infrastructure and compute platforms that researchers use for their work. You do not need to be an AI or computational research domain expert to deliver world-class infrastructure, but you do need to quickly obtain a deep technical understanding of new domains. You should enjoy being self-directed and identifying the most important problems to solve as the team matures with standardized tools and processes around stability, robust service delivery and scaling. This role supports hybrid working arrangements to provide flexibility. While the Centre's operational needs require successful candidates to start as soon as possible, we are able to accommodate individual notice periods to attract top talent. What will you be doing?: As a member of the AI Supercomputing Team, you will; Use tools such as Python, Rust, Terraform / OpenTofu, Kubernetes, Git and Bash. Design and operate large, highly available supercomputing services managed as software-defined infrastructures, and integrated as complete computational experiments. You will experience designing and operating massive-scale GPU and combined CPU/GPU workloads across these services. You will design and debug platforms, and will work closely with researchers as you co-design solutions that will enable the development and operation of new algorithms and software to solve leading-edge research problems. You should apply if: Want to help build, maintain and secure some of the largest, modern software-defined supercomputing systems. Would enjoy working with world class domain and AI researchers as your primary workload. Have built small to large clusters or dabbled in building your own physical or software-defined systems, and have motivation to scale up to something massive and nationally impactful . Love building large distributed, highly available systems, and want to see them used for truly open national-scale research in a cybersecurity compliant manner. For our AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Engineer role focusing on storage and networking, you’ll need: Domain expertise in 1 or more areas from NetOps and DevOps. Degree (or equivalent practical experience) in computer science, computational or ML/AI research or in a natural science with a high degree of competence in computer science or computational research. Good organisational skills to manage not just your own workload but also that of less experienced members of the team. The available job description provides a full view of the person specification. Additional information: For any informal enquiries, please contact Emma Rose, Centre Manager - emma.rose@bristol.ac.uk. Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until August 2030. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Positions available: x3 FTE Grade: K Salary: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum. School/Unit: Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 31st January 2027 Please note that applications will be reviewed and shortlisted on a rolling basis throughout the advertising period. Interviews will also be held at regular intervals during this time. We reserve the right to close the advert early once the vacancies have been filled, therefore early applications are encouraged. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391638": {
      "title": "Catering Supervisor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113847 The role: Source catering is the University of Bristol’s award-winning catering brand and are looking for an enthusiastic and friendly individual to take the role of Catering Supervisor at the university. The role will be working in one of our catering outlets which include 4 catered halls of residence, events & conference spaces, 9 cafes, 2 bars and a food court and will suit someone who has experience in the industry and can demonstrate leadership qualities. This is an amazing opportunity that provides work-life balance, training & development, working alongside a large and supportive team and involvement in exciting food & drink projects. The contract is 35 hours per week, working 5 days out of 7 with a generous amount of time off throughout the year. Our Catering Supervisors will look after daily tasks, ensuring our team have what they need to do their job on shift and setting the tone of friendly and professional customer service What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will focus on leading the front of house catering team members, setting an example of great customer service whilst supporting the managers with some of the daily tasks like ordering, rotas, end of day duties and due diligence. Setting an example, great communication, organisation and a genuine interest in food & drink will be key to your success. You should apply if: Hospitality experience in food service and dealing with guests Supervisory experience Experience of working within a diverse team High organisational skills and the ability to adapt to the needs of the business Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours Shift pattern: 5 days over 7 Grade: D Salary: £24,685 - £26,093 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Catering & Events Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Thursday 22nd October For informal queries please contact: Ioana Dobos ( Unit Manager ) – ioana.dobos@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391597": {
      "title": "Cleaner (Langford)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113816 The role: Working early mornings, in the small friendly cleaning team at the Langford Campus, BS40 5DU, (14 miles from central Bristol), you will be responsible for the presentation of a variety of facilities and rooms for students, staff and veterinary services buildings. What will you be doing?: You will undertake cleaning duties such as: dusting, mopping, sweeping, vacuuming, and washroom cleaning within University properties, following departmental procedures that meet specified standards. You should apply if: No experience is necessary as training will be provided. You must be hard working, flexible and have a helpful nature towards customers. You must be able to have transport to work at the Langford Campus, North Somerset for 6am start. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time - 25 hours per week Shift pattern: Early morning start, 6am Grade: B Salary: £24,479 pro rata School/Unit: Campus Division - Site Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Tuesday 20th October For informal queries please contact: Kelsey Hatcher (Cleaning Supervisor) on 07765334312 or kelsey.hatcher@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391528": {
      "title": "Commercial Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Department: Finance Services Reference: SUPP113770 The role: We have a thrilling opportunity to join the University of Bristol as an experienced Commercial Contracts Officer in the Procurement Team. We’re looking for a candidate who can provide expertise in supplier management which evidences ability to influence and drive decision-making, commercial acumen and have experience of managing contracts through the contract lifecycle . As Commercial Contracts Officer you will collaborate closely with Suppliers, building managers, and operational teams to help ensure our facilities and buildings are maintained to the highest compliance and safety standards. This supports the University vision of providing world-class research and teaching spaces. You will report into the Commercial Contracts Manager, which forms part of a newly created Commercial and Contract management discipline. You will work closely together to provide contract specific advice, support and updates to Departmental Heads and Managers in Property Infrastructure Teams. You will also partner with colleagues in wider Campus Services and in the Procurement Team, with the aim of ensuring we have high performing frameworks and contracts, that deliver and exceed contractual commitments with our suppliers / contractors. The University has expanded its portfolio with the new Enterprise Campus Sloane Robinson Building in Temple Quarter. This role will proactively support the management of the Hard Facilities and compliance servicing contract and provide contractual expertise for escalation of contractual issues internally and from Supplier. Upon joining the University, you will have access to excellent staff benefits, competitive salaries, a generous annual leave entitlement (including bank holidays and University closure days), volunteer days, access to training opportunities and pension schemes. Hybrid working: Expectation to work from office 2 days per week, and as and when for in-person engagements. What will you be doing?: Contract lifecycle activities, including mobilisation, onboarding suppliers, creation of contract management plans, stakeholder management, leading supplier review meetings, contract extension, exit and termination. Work closely with the Commercial Contracts Manager to help manage and report on contract performance, including scorecards, risk reviews, and providing solutions for successful contract delivery. Promoting best practice with contract management practices and providing commercial expertise and support to stakeholders, to help drive value for money and benefits aligned to business plans. Ensuring contractual commitments and stakeholder needs are met, holding suppliers to account on performance, time and cost, via KPI performance monitoring. Co-ordinate change negotiations and variations to ensure contract changes are tracked and conditions updated. You should apply if: Thorough proven experience working within contract management, procurement related discipline, and strong communication and negotiation skills to drive positive outcomes and influence decisions. You can demonstrate business acumen / commercial awareness, and can assess financial, operational and strategic risks and support mitigation strategies and deliver cost improvements. You have experience of supporting implementation of complex contracts across multiple sites and suppliers through building strong relationships. You are a self-starter, with a growth mindset, able to work with minimal direction to resolve complex issues and also a supportive team player, able to provide new ideas and solutions. Excellent analytical, organisational, and IT skills, to interpret performance data and report on findings. You are educated to degree level or equivalent relevant experience in Contract Management, Supplier Management, Asset Management. Some knowledge and experience of using contractual terms and conditions (construction contracts such as JCT / NEC would be advantageous) Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time, 35 hours per week Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: Finance Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 14th October 2026 For informal queries please contact Steph Robinson, Commercial Contract Manager via email: steph.robinson@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391655": {
      "title": "Core Facilities Technician (Teaching and Research)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113822 The role: We are seeking applications for the role of Core Facilities Technician (Teaching and Research) for the School of Psychology and Neuroscience. You will provide technical support, training and specialist advice to staff and students in Psychology and Neuroscience relating to specialist software, experimental equipment and laboratory safety. You will deliver software and hardware workshops for UG and PGT students and provide day-to-day technical support for teaching and research activities. You will support teaching and research activities in compliance with Health and Safety and regulatory requirements, providing training and guidance on specialised techniques, software and equipment. What will you be doing?: You will: Hold delegated responsibility for elements of Health and Safety such as reviewing student and staff research risk assessments as well as production of up-to-date Standard Operating Procedures under the direction of the Technical Manager to ensure compliance with relevant health and safety requirements. Support the efficient and safe running of the Psychology and Neuroscience research laboratories across the Priory Road complex and Biomedical Sciences Building, including Health and Safety and compliance with the relevant regulatory policies and procedures. Assist in the development, programming and use of specialised equipment, software and processes in research and teaching. Support and/or deliver workshops, troubleshooting sessions and induction talks for UG, PGT and PGR students. Support Widening Participation, community engagement and research events. Undertake or co-ordinate maintaining, upgrading, optimising and integrating of laboratory equipment. Support academic staff in the testing of participants as part of ongoing research or teaching within the School. Support the development of systems and maintain records of laboratory and equipment bookings, and equipment maintenance. Support staff and student IT/ AV queries as required Hold responsibility for staff and student induction into specialised spaces. Undertake any other tasks that may reasonably be requested by the relevant Technical Manager/s or School Head of Operations. You should apply if: You have: Relevant technical skills or work experience in Psychology and Neuroscience, or a related discipline. A demonstrable understanding of Psychology teaching and research laboratory environments. An understanding of qualitative and quantitative research methods in psychology. Experience with research software and laboratory technologies relevant to Psychology and Neuroscience. A proven ability to structure and deliver curriculum support sessions on technical issues. Knowledge of British Psychological Society (BPS) guidelines and procedures for human research. Experience in providing technical support to undergraduate and/or postgraduate students. Knowledge of current health and safety regulations and safe working practices in Psychology and/or neuroscience disciplines. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full-time Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Thursday 22nd October 2026 Interviews are anticipated to be held on Monday 2nd November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Natalie Griffiths-Stubbs, Technical Manager via email: Natalie.griffiths@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391555": {
      "title": "Dental Therapist Tutor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108708 The role: We are seeking exceptional Dental Therapists to join our team and deliver outstanding education and team training to our Dental Hygiene and Therapy students at Bristol Dental School. Working within your Scope of Practice you will be responsible for the delivery of academic and clinical teaching to our undergraduates, working in an integrated teaching environment. What will you be doing?: Part of our education strategy is to have a strong emphasis on integrating learning across all disciplines and team training. The appointee will initially be responsible for the clinical supervision of Dental Therapy (BDT) and Dental Hygiene (BDH) students. For BDT/BDH students this concept starts in the Clinical Skills Simulation Clinic and culminates in clinical placements within the Community and clinics in our purpose-built General Practice Suite alongside Final year BDS students. The appointee will develop other teaching and learning activities, working alongside academic colleagues to deliver an outstanding student experience. You should apply if: The successful candidate would ideally have experience of teaching, this may be at a practice-level, but should be able to demonstrate a passion and enthusiasm for teaching. The appointee will have excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with an ability to foster collaborative approaches across clinical disciplines. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time Grade: J Salary: £44,746 – 50,253 p.a (pro rata) School/Unit: Bristol Dental School Shift pattern: 16 hours per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 18/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Sarah Bain sarah.bain@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391647": {
      "title": "Deputy Business Development Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113768 The role: This is a key role within the Business Development Team, supporting the Business Development Manager in leading a centralised team that delivers a wide range of commercial and business services across the division. The team plays a vital role in generating income to support and enhance the student experience, with responsibility for delivering an annual income target of approx. £3 million. It leads on business development activities including marketing and communications, facilities hire and booking management, event delivery, systems development and support, customer relationship management, financial management and administration services. As Deputy Business Development Manager, you will help drive commercial performance, support the development of new opportunities, and ensure the effective delivery of high-quality services. Working closely with colleagues across the division, you will provide leadership, operational oversight and customer-focused support, helping teams achieve their objectives and contribute to the division's overall success. What will you be doing?: As Deputy Business Development Manager, you will lead key areas of the Business Development team's portfolio, with responsibility for income generation, customer management, and systems development. You will identify opportunities for continuous improvement and lead initiatives that enhance service delivery, customer experience, and commercial performance. A significant part of the role will involve leading our Memberships Team, which generates more than half of the division's annual income. You will support the team in achieving ambitious targets, oversee membership growth and retention, and work closely with Front of House colleagues to resolve escalated customer issues and ensure a consistently high-quality customer experience. You will build and maintain strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders to support the management, development, and implementation of divisional systems, ensuring compliance with university policies, procedures, and relevant legislation. Current systems include Legend Leisure Management System (including the Innovatise app), CoursePro, Develop, and MyERP. Using business intelligence and performance data, you will provide insight and recommendations to support decision-making and drive improvements across the division. This may include analysing engagement and participation trends, membership sales performance, facility utilisation, customer behaviour, and progress against income and service delivery KPIs. Working closely with the Business Development Manager, you will help ensure the division achieves its commercial objectives and service standards. You will contribute to strategic planning and policy development where required and provide leadership support by deputising for the Business Development Manager at meetings and key stakeholder forums. You should apply if: Experience of leading, developing, or supervising teams, with a commitment to supporting colleague development and delivering high-quality services. Significant experience working within business development, commercial operations, customer service, membership management, or a related area. Experience of identifying and implementing process improvements, with strong analytical and problem-solving skills. The ability to interpret complex data and translate insights into clear recommendations for a range of audiences. Experience of using customer feedback and performance data to drive service improvements and enhance the customer experience. Experience of working with leisure management systems and supporting systems development, implementation, or optimisation. Experience of planning and facilitating workshops, focus groups, training, or stakeholder engagement activities. Strong stakeholder management skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and promote best practice. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Sport, Exercise & Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 22/10/2026 For informal queries please contact Jo Blackett 07989 158548 jo.blackett@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391221": {
      "title": "EPSRC Postdoctoral Pathway Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108703 The role: The EPSRC Postdoctoral Pathway supports EPSRC-funded PhD graduates who are excited about taking the first steps towards research independence with a two-year Fellowship at the University of Bristol. The scheme offers the opportunity to launch your own, independent research career in an exciting and innovative research environment. Postdoctoral Pathway Fellows will propose their own bespoke programme to conduct innovative research at the frontiers of Science and Engineering. Project proposals are invited across the full range of topics in the EPSRC research portfolio EPSRC strategy, remit and programmes – UKRI . Successful applicants will have the chance to take their research to the next level, whether by delivering high-impact original research, or by exploiting new interdisciplinary research opportunities. The Postdoctoral Pathway award should not be viewed as an opportunity to complete PhD work. What will you be doing?: Successful applicants will receive funding for up to 2 years. Benefits include: Work within world-class research groups at the University of Bristol In addition to a Project Sponsor (a Bristol academic staff member supporting your project proposal), you will have access to an academic mentor for advice on research and career development Access to personal and professional development opportunities. Postdoctoral Pathway Fellows will be recruited at Grade J on the University of Bristol salary scale and receive a grant of £5,000 for research costs. You should apply if: This scheme is designed to support Early Career Researchers who are nearing completion of their PhD or have recently completed it. Eligibility criteria: All applicants must have received PhD studentship support from EPSRC (stipend and/or fees) or training award, for example CDT, ICASE, or IDLA studentship Applicants must not hold a permanent academic position Applicants must have submitted their PhD thesis before the post begins and meet either of the following two requirements: Applicants who have recently completed their PhD they must have had their viva on or after 1st November 2025 (12 months prior to the application deadline) Applicants who are near completion of their PhD must intend to submit their doctoral thesis on or before 31st January 2027 (within 3 months of the application deadline). If they do not submit their thesis by this date, the award offer will be withdrawn. Applicants who are yet to submit must include a statement of support from their PhD supervisor confirming their expected submission timeline. Additional information: Whilst this vacancy is administratively linked to the School of Engineering Mathematics & Technology (SEMT), applications are welcomed from eligible candidates across all Schools To apply for this role please download and complete the attached documents (as specified in the Application Guidance). Alongside completing the other sections of the application, the additional documents should be uploaded to the ‘Other documents' section of the application portal. Please see the Application Guidance for more details. Please contact doctoral-prize@bristol.ac.uk with any queries. Contract type: Fixed term up to 24 months Work pattern: Full time Grade: J/Pathway 2 Salary: £44,746 - £50253 per annum plus a a grant of £5,000 for research costs School/Unit: School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 The interview date is anticipated to take place on Thursday 10th December 2026 For informal queries please contact: Laura Maclean, Senior Research Administrator Email: EPSRC-Postdoctoral-Pathway@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391500": {
      "title": "Faculty Head of Technical Services (FSE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113758 The role: Shape the technical services that enable world-leading science and engineering This is an opportunity to lead a large and diverse technical community, influence Faculty strategy and ensure that outstanding people, facilities and services enable excellent research, education, innovation and external partnership. As Faculty Head of Technical Services, you will provide visible, strategic leadership for all technical services across the Faculty of Science and Engineering. Reporting to the Director of Faculty Operations and working as part of the Faculty Leadership Team, you will shape a complex service spanning diverse scientific and engineering disciplines, teams, specialist facilities, infrastructure, budgets and risks. You will ensure that technical services are safe, resilient, efficient and ready for the future. More than maintaining excellent operations, you will bring technical insight into senior decision-making, influence research and education priorities, create opportunities for collaboration and innovation, and help the Faculty make the best use of its people, facilities and resources. The role offers substantial scope to lead change. You will develop technical capability and culture, guide workforce and infrastructure planning, build strong partnerships across schools and professional services, and represent the Faculty and University internally and externally. You will also contribute to University-wide technical leadership and may deputise for the Director of Faculty Operations when required. Who will thrive here? A credible and inclusive senior leader who can see the whole system: people, safety, service quality, infrastructure, finance, research, education and long-term capability. You will be comfortable making difficult priorities clear, influencing at senior level and leading through complexity without losing sight of staff and student experience. What will you be doing?: Lead people, culture and change: Provide visible leadership to a large, multidisciplinary technical community. Build a culture of accountability, inclusion, innovation, collaboration, performance and continuous improvement while leading significant change and strategic projects. Shape technical services for the future: Design, evaluate and continuously improve a complex service model that meets current and future research, education and operational needs, including workforce planning, resilience and service quality. Direct facilities, infrastructure and investment: Set priorities for technical facilities, infrastructure projects, specialist equipment and purchases. Develop strong business cases and ensure resources are used effectively. Assure safety, risk and continuity: Lead operational health and safety, regulatory compliance, risk management and business continuity, creating clear ownership and confidence across the Faculty. Influence Faculty and University strategy: Bring authoritative technical insight to senior decision-making, contribute to the Faculty Leadership Team and University-wide initiatives, and represent Bristol with internal and external partners. Build partnerships and unlock value: Develop strong relationships with academic and professional-services leaders. Create collaboration across schools and faculties, support innovation and identify commercial, grant-funded and revenue opportunities. You should apply if: Strategic leadership and change: Significant senior leadership and management experience, including visible leadership of highly diverse technical teams and delivery of complex organisational change. Technical credibility: Highly developed expertise in a relevant technical discipline, together with sufficient breadth to understand different technical environments and turn evidence into effective strategic solutions. Safety, risk and resilience: Substantial experience of operational safety, business continuity, regulatory compliance, risk management and constructive conflict resolution. Finance, planning and improvement: Experience of planning and controlling substantial budgets, prioritising competing demands, building business cases and delivering measurable continuous improvement. Influence and inclusive people leadership: The ability to direct, negotiate and influence across a complex stakeholder environment, and to engage, motivate and inspire teams with diverse perspectives and priorities. Professional standing: Knowledge gained through a relevant degree, professional qualification or registration, supported by significant professional experience, plus management or leadership training. Professional registration and a NEBOSH Certificate are desirable. We are interested in evidence of impact and transferable leadership. Your experience may have been gained in higher education, a research institute, a national laboratory, life sciences, advanced manufacturing, engineering or another complex technical environment. For a full view of the requirements and responsbilities, please refer to the job description here . Additional information: Why Bristol The University of Bristol exists to make a positive impact locally, nationally and globally through distinctive education, innovative research, excellence, inclusivity and partnership. Its 2030 vision is to be firmly established among the world’s top 50 great research-intensive universities and the UK top 10. The Faculty of Science and Engineering’s 2025-2030 strategy brings together nine schools around a shared mission: developing and applying world-leading scientific knowledge, understanding and technology to address global challenges and improve lives. The Faculty is prioritising interdisciplinary research, innovative education, strong partnerships and investment in its most promising areas. This is a particularly exciting time to join. The Faculty’s strengths include advanced computation and quantum technology, AI and data science, digital chemistry, energy and environment, health and living, materials and manufacturing, networks and security, and novel materials. Major infrastructure and innovation include the University’s investment connected to the Isambard-AI supercomputer and the new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, which is opening in 2026 as a collaborative hub for research, education, enterprise and civic partnership. Bristol also offers a strong employment proposition. University benefits include pension provision, learning and development, wellbeing support, staff networks, sustainable travel benefits and relocation support for eligible staff. Work-life balance provisions include a typical 35-hour week, flexible-working options, 25 days of annual leave, seven University closure days and eight bank holidays, subject to the relevant policies and the requirements of the role. Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Grade: M Salary: £77,537 - £91,082 School/Unit: The Faculty of Science and Engineering This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday, 18th October. For informal queries please contact: Dani Murtagh, Director of Faculty Operations (Faculty of Science and Engineering) - dani.murtagh@bristol.ac.uk. The anticipated date for the selection process is Thursday, 5th November. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391715": {
      "title": "Health and Fitness Adviser",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113844 The role: You will play a key role in supporting our Health and Fitness facilities, helping customers lead happier and healthier lives. Responsibilities include supervising the gym, free weights area, and exercise studios to ensure all users can exercise in a safe, clean, welcoming, and enjoyable environment. The role also involves carrying out administrative tasks, maintaining accurate membership records, and ensuring all relevant documentation is up to date. You will be encouraged to develop your knowledge of health, fitness, and exercise through continuous self-directed learning. As part of a varied and flexible work programme, you will deliver support across a range of services, including exercise classes, Health Minds sessions, personal training activities, and gym floor supervision within our Health & Fitness facilities. Flexibility and a customer-focused approach are essential to successfully meet the needs of our members and visitors. What will you be doing?: You will undertake a varied role supporting customers across our Health & Fitness facilities. Responsibilities will include gym floor supervision, maintaining high standards of cleanliness, delivering fitness classes, providing personal training support, and assisting within specialist programmes, including our Healthy Minds service. You should apply if: You have: Experience working in a fitness environment Enthusiasm to help members Recognised fitness qualification Good communication skills Ability to deliver group fitness classes Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: 20 hours per week Grade: E Salary: £26,093 - £28,778 per annum School/Unit: Centre for Sport, Exercise and Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 26th October 2026 For informal queries please contact James.Rowntree@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391559": {
      "title": "Heilbronn Research Fellowships in Pure Mathematics, Data Science, and Quantum Computing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108722 The role: The Heilbronn Institute for Mathematical Research invites applications for Heilbronn Research Fellowships. Fellows divide their time equally between their own research and the classified research programme of the Heilbronn Institute. Research areas of interest include, but are not restricted to, Algebra, Algebraic Geometry, Combinatorics, Data Science, Number Theory, Probability, and Quantum Computing. Fellows have previously been appointed with backgrounds in most areas of Pure Mathematics and Statistics, and Mathematical/Theoretical Physics. We expect to make up to 10 appointments at the University of Bristol , Imperial College London , King’s College London , UCL , and the University of Manchester . These appointments will mainly be in Pure Mathematics with up to two of the appointments at the University of Bristol in Data Science or Quantum Computing. The salary range is £46,874 - £52,517 (or local equivalent) depending on previous experience, plus the London weighting where appropriate. There is a salary supplement of £3.5K pa, in recognition of the distinctive nature of these Fellowships. Payment of this supplement is conditional on a PhD thesis having been accepted in final form. The positions are 3 years with fixed-term funding until 2030 with a preferred start date of 1 October 2027. Extensions of up to three years may be available, which can be held at a wider range of universities in the UK. What will you be doing?: Fellows divide their time equally between their own research and the classified research programme of the Heilbronn Institute, thus enjoying opportunities to engage in collaborative work as well as individual projects. Fellows join a cohort of around 50 Heilbronn Research Fellows that includes some of the leading early-career mathematicians in the UK. Expert mentoring will be provided by the host department, with a national programme of career development events overseen by our Associate Chairs, who are based in the School of Mathematics at the University of Bristol, Imperial College, and the University of Manchester. The internal research programme is determined by the Head of HIMR in conjunction with GCHQ. Fellows are presented with a range of challenging mathematical problems and are given the facilities and support to contribute to one or more of them, frequently as part of a collaborative team. You should apply if: Due to the nature of the Institute's work, Fellows must apply for a national security clearance at an appropriate level before appointment. Applications are open only to UK residents who are UK, or dual UK nationals : applicants are welcome to contact the Heilbronn Manager at himr-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk to discuss eligibility before applying. The Heilbronn Institute is a supporter of the LMS Good Practice Scheme aimed at advancing women’s careers in mathematics and we particularly welcome applications from women. Additional information: Applications will be made through mathjobs.org Applicants will need to submit a cover letter, indicating the institutions where they prefer to hold their Fellowship from the above list of partner universities. In addition, we require a CV and a one-page statement of external research objectives over the 3 year Fellowship term. Applicants must arrange for three letters of reference to be uploaded to the mathjobs.org website, prior to the closing date. The application deadline is 11.59pm GMT, Sunday 8 th November 2026. We anticipate holding interviews over the 11 th - 14th of January 2027. For informal queries please contact: himr-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391689": {
      "title": "HR Shared Services Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Human Resources Reference: SUPP113801 The role: This is an exciting opportunity for you to make a real difference within the Employee Services Hub to play a pivotal role in the HR shared services administrative operation, delivering a confidential, efficient, and effective professional HR and administrative support service. This role will allow you to explore the opportunities that a career in HR can deliver. What will you be doing?: You will provide key support as first point of contact for a range of day-to-day queries from internal and external customers. You will be responsible for end-to-end administration across employment lifecycle activity, focusing on onboarding and employee life cycle tasks, providing advice and guidance on HR processes and procedures. You should apply if: You have proven experience in a high volume, busy administrative or HR transactional role, as well as excellent Office 365 skills, a quick learner with the ability to pick up new systems and processes with ease You will be a natural problem solver, always looking for the answer yourself before asking how to solve an issue You have excellent organisational, time management and administrative skills, including the ability to manage and prioritise a busy workload to meet deadlines You have excellent interpersonal skills, a positive customer-focused approach and are a great team player You enjoy working in a busy environment and can respond positively to change Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term Contract - Maternity cover for 12 months Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum School/Unit: Human Resources - Employee Services Hub This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Sunday 25th October For informal queries please contact: Sharon Faulkner (Shared Services Team Leader) on Sharon.Faulkner@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391537": {
      "title": "Leading Cleaner",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113739 The role: The University of Campus Division has part-time opportunities for enthusiastic and motivated people to work in a number of busy and active cleaning roles. You will be required to work as part of a team and also on your own to deliver the high standards required by the University. What will you be doing?: Expected to maintain a high standard of cleanliness within academic departments, laboratories, libraries, and office areas in order to maintain a clean, welcoming, and safe environment for our students, staff, and visitors. Leading the cleaning team by example and assisting the Cleaning Supervisor with training, organising and similar tasks. Deputising for them when necessary. You should apply if: Previous cleaning experience is preferred but not essential as full training will be given. Previous experience of leading a small team is preferred but not essential. Benefits of working for the University 25 days of bookable annual leave per year plus 7 University closure days Automatic entry to a generous pension scheme Inclusive culture, valuing the diversity of thought, beliefs, and background. Staff discounts including annual bus fares, University gym and cycle to work scheme. Opportunities to build your skills and to progress your career. Supportive and rewarding working environment. Occasional paid overtime available. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time - 21.5 hours per week Shift pattern: 07:00-11:30 Monday to Thursday 07:00-10:30 Friday Grade: C Salary: £24,479 - £24,685 per annum pro rata (hourly rate £13.45 - £13.56) School/Unit: Campus Division - Site Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Thursday 15 October For informal queries please contact: the Site Services Department on 0117 4283140. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391676": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108476 The role: The School of Computer Science at the University of Bristol is seeking to grow our thriving and talented AI community by hiring a new AI lecturer. We have a long-standing track record for world-leading AI, and recent successes place us at the forefront of UK AI research and teaching. Bristol hosts Isambard AI, the UK’s national AI supercomputing facility, leads two of the UK’s nine UKRI National AI Research Hubs (in Collective Intelligence and Information Theory), runs back-to-back UKRI Centres for Doctoral Training in Interactive AI and Practice-Oriented AI, and has recently launched an innovative undergraduate degree in Computer Science with AI. Bristol’s world-class AI research spans applied and theoretical work in intelligent agents, machine learning, computer vision, reasoning, and reinforcement learning, and tackles problems in multimodal and interactive AI. We solve interdisciplinary challenges in collaboration with researchers in creative technologies, the environmental and life sciences, and robotics. We encourage applicants from any area of AI to apply and are specifically interested in candidates with strengths in Multi-Modal AI and Computer Vision, and those that bring new kinds of AI expertise to the School. The School values diversity and is committed to creating an inclusive environment where all staff can thrive. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who wish to work part-time, to job-share, or who require flexible working patterns. We are happy to discuss these possibilities with candidates at any stage of the recruitment process.‑time, job‑share, or who require flexible working patterns. We are happy to discuss these possibilities with candidates at any stage of the recruitment process What will you be doing?: The postholder will contribute to the University’s impressive record of world-leading research (as demonstrated in REF 2021 which ranked the School 7th in the UK for CS research) and to developing and delivering the School’s education programmes (including our new undergraduate degree in Computer Science with AI). The School prides itself on its academic quality and its collegiality. We are incredibly proud of our outstanding working environment with a dedicated professional services team, a very high standard of achievement in research and education, and a culture of inclusivity and kindness. You should apply if: Candidates will have a PhD in Computer Science or a closely related discipline (or equivalent industrial experience) and a track record of publishing in top academic venues and applying for external funding appropriate to their career stage. The potential for leadership, undertaking research collaborations with colleagues from top international labs, and collaborating on impactful translation of ideas with relevant industrial partners is desirable. The successful candidate will be able to design and deliver excellent teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate level and will be committed to making the most of growing opportunities in this area. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open Ended Work pattern: Full-time Grade: J or K Salary: Grade J £43,482 - £53,253 per annum or Grade K £50,253 - £58,225 per annum School/Unit: The School of Computer Science Advert/selection timeline: This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Friday 6th November 2026 Shortlisting: Tuesday 24 th November Interviews: Presentations Thursday 3 rd December and interview panel Friday 4 th December For informal queries please contact: Dima Damen, dima.damen@bristol.ac.uk and Seth Bullock, seth.bullock@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391455": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Pure Mathematics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108709 The role: The School of Mathematics is seeking to appoint a Lecturer (analogous to Assistant Professor) or Senior Lecturer in Pure Mathematics, with the level of appointment depending on the experience of the successful candidate. We are looking for a world-class researcher in Logic or Set Theory. We interpret these areas broadly, and although preference would be given to a candidate in set theory, it is not limited to such, and candidates in other areas of modern logic, such as computability theory, model theory and proof theory, are encouraged to apply. What will you be doing?: The post-holder will be affiliated with the School’s Institute for Pure Mathematics and will be responsible for their own research programme, undertaking independent research. The post-holder will also teach undergraduate and postgraduate students and take on appropriate administrative duties. They will be a key member of the Logic and Set Theory Group at Bristol, which is an internationally leading centre for research in set theory and the philosophy and foundations of mathematics. You should apply if: You have a passion for developing and leading innovative and impactful research in Logic and Set Theory. You have demonstrated the self-motivation, initiative, and organisational skills required to carry out a successful research programme. You have a proven publication record in internationally recognised, peer-reviewed scientific journals. Additional information: References: It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure that three reference letters are sent to maths-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk by 23:59 on Sunday 1st November 2026 . Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full time/100% time Grade: J, K or L Salary: Grade J in the range £44,746 – £50,253 or Grade K in the range £51,753 – £58,225 or Grade L in the £58,225 – £69,488, per annum (appointed at grade depending on experience) School/Unit: School of Mathematics This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 It is expected that the final selection process will be held in November 2026 For informal queries please contact one of the following contacts: Prof Oliver Johnson, Head of School, headofschool-maths@bristol.ac.uk Prof Tim Burness, Professor of Pure Mathematics Director of the Institute for Pure Mathematics, t.burness@bristol.ac.uk Prof Philip Welch, Professor of Pure Mathematics p.welch@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391584": {
      "title": "Philanthropy Officer (Fixed-term contract)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Global Engagement Reference: SUPP113660 The role: Philanthropic support creates meaningful opportunities for students and researchers at the University of Bristol. The Philanthropy Officer will work in the Leadership Giving team, responsible for cultivating strong relationships with existing and potential donors to raise funds for University priorities in the £1,000 - £10,000 range, by contacting Bristol alumni and friends with tailored communications (post, phone and digital) and creating inspirational updates and fundraising asks with the help of colleagues. This role can be considered on a part-time or full-time basis, between 0.6 and 1.0 FTE. Applicants should state their preferred FTE arrangement in their cover letter. Part-time hours will be calculated on a pro rata basis (for example, 0.6 FTE equates to 21 hours per week). Please note that this role is a fixed-term contract ending on 12th October 2027. What will you be doing?: You will help with the operational delivery of effective fundraising and stewardship to inspire donations at Leadership Giving level (£1,000-£10,000). This includes: Creating personalised fundraising and engagement communications to inspire our Leadership Giving donors and potential donors Building relationships with individual supporters through tailored emails and conversations Working closely with colleagues in our Regular Giving and Individual Philanthropy teams to support to secure donations and steward donors effectively Collaboration with colleagues across our engagement, events, data, insight, marketing and communications teams Project management of our US Leadership Giving activity Evaluating and analysing Leadership Giving fundraising activity results You should apply if: You have experience working in fundraising or donor relations and have the confidence to make direct philanthropic asks You have excellent organisation skills, you can juggle multiple projects and deadlines, and have a strong attention to detail. You can communicate excellently in writing and verbally. You have the ability to build positive working relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, both internal and external You are passionate about supporting Bristol students from low-income backgrounds, enabling ground-breaking research and have a belief in the crucial role universities play in society. For a full view of the responsibilities and requirements, please refer to the attached job description. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed-term contract until 12/10/2027. If you are an internal applicant and currently hold a substantive post and would like to consider this opportunity on a secondment basis, this is welcomed. Please ensure you have discussed this with your current line manager before applying. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Though the role can be considered on a part-time basis (minimum 0.6 FTE). Hybrid working arrangement: Team day working on campus is a Wednesday each week, however we are often in the office more often due to events taking place on various days of the week. Grade: H Salary: £35,608 - £39,906 per annum (pro-rated for part time) School/Unit: Global Engagement Division (GED) For informal queries please contact: Alicia Jago - Deputy Director (Fundraising and Stewardship) - Alicia.Jago@bristol.ac.uk. The anticipated interview date is the 2nd November This advert will close on Sunday, 18th October at 23.59pm UK time. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=390478": {
      "title": "Professor of Cyber Security",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108475 The role: The University of Bristol is seeking to appoint an outstanding Professor of Cyber Security to join the internationally recognised Bristol Cyber Security Group (BCSG) in the School of Computer Science. This is an exceptional opportunity for an established academic leader to shape the future of cyber security research and education within one of the UK's leading research-intensive universities. We are looking for an internationally recognised scholar whose research aligns with and complements the strengths of the BCSG and who can provide strategic leadership, inspire colleagues and students, and deliver world-leading research with real-world impact. BCSG is one of the UK's largest and most influential cyber security research communities, bringing together researchers across cyber-physical systems, software security, usable security and privacy, and cybercrime and online harms. The Group has a strong track record of securing major research funding, influencing policy and practice, and delivering impactful interdisciplinary research in collaboration with industry, government and international partners. What will you be doing?: As Professor of Cyber Security, you will: Lead and deliver internationally recognised research in an area aligned with the BCSG. Develop an ambitious research vision and secure significant external funding. Build and lead high-performing research teams and collaborative partnerships. Contribute to the strategic direction and growth of cyber security research within the School and University. Deliver innovative teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Supervise and mentor PhD students, postdoctoral researchers and academic colleagues. Play a leading role in fostering an inclusive, collaborative and intellectually vibrant academic community. You should apply if: You can demonstrate: An international reputation for research excellence in cyber security, in an area aligned with BCSG. A track record of publication in leading conferences and peer-reviewed journals, or equivalent industrial research leadership. A sustained record of securing external research funding and leading significant research programmes. Experience of teaching and supervising undergraduate and postgraduate students. A proven ability to lead, develop and grow a research area, team or community. A commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and delivering research with academic, societal and industrial impact. For a full view of the requirements and responsibilities, please refer to the attached candidate brochure. Why Bristol? This is an exciting time to join the University of Bristol. As a member of the Russell Group, Bristol is recognised globally for the quality and impact of its research and education. The University is ranked among the world's leading universities and is committed to ambitious growth in research, innovation and global engagement. You will join a School of Computer Science that is expanding significantly and forms a key part of the University's new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, a landmark development designed to bring together researchers, students, industry and public sector partners to tackle some of society's biggest challenges. BCSG is a flagship tenant in Temple Quarter, occupying its new bespoke facility in August 2026, complete with CNI lab, dedicated IoT lab and secure data centre. BCSG offers a uniquely supportive and collaborative environment, with over 80 researchers working across multiple disciplines and a strong portfolio of major research programmes, doctoral training initiatives and industry partnerships. Researchers benefit from extensive connections with government, the National Cyber Security Centre, UKRI, international collaborators and leading organisations across the technology sector. You'll also be part of a university driving innovation at scale, with access to world-class facilities and opportunities for collaboration across areas including AI, data science, digital futures, engineering, social sciences and policy. Bristol is home to the UK's flagship AI supercomputing infrastructure, helping to position the University at the forefront of digital and technological research. Located in one of the UK's most vibrant and creative cities, Bristol offers an outstanding quality of life, a thriving technology ecosystem, excellent transport links and a strong culture of innovation and entrepreneurship. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: M - Professorial Range 1 Salary: £74,828 - £77,537 per annum School/Unit: Computer Science This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 Shortlisting will take place week commencing the 2nd November 2026 Interviews are anticipated to take place week commencing the 16th or 23rd November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Mike Fraser, Mike.Fraser@bristol.ac.uk Inah Omoronyia, inah.omoronyia@bristol.ac.uk Awais Rashid, awais.rashid@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391650": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108724 The role: We have an exciting opportunity for a talented systematic reviewer or a quantitative researcher with an interest in evidence synthesis to join the BEAM (Bristol Evidence Synthesis, Appraisal and Modelling) Centre at the University of Bristol. BEAM brings together researchers working on evidence synthesis and modelling research across Bristol Medical School. BEAM is an internationally renowned centre of excellence in evidence synthesis and is the only centre to host all three types of NIHR funded Evidence Synthesis Groups (ESGs), providing access to a wealth of knowledge and expertise in evidence synthesis across a broad range of topics and clinical areas. You will work primarily within the NIHR Bristol ESG and NIHR BUcKLES ESG but with some flexibility to contribute to the work of other research groups within BEAM where needed. The Bristol ESG produces evidence reviews on a wide range of topics for the NIHR. The BUcKLES group is the only ESG dedicated to undertaking living systematic reviews. You will be part of a multidisciplinary team including systematic reviewers, statisticians, information specialists, qualitative researchers, and health economists. You will also work closely with clinical experts and public contributors. The post is available full time from the 1 st November 2026 until 31/03/2030, but part-time working will be considered at a minimum of 0.6 FTE. Requests for flexible working patterns will be considered. Hybrid working is available: we encourage staff to work in the office as face-to-face working and regular contact with colleagues are key to a healthy workplace. The postholder would be expected to attend the office a minimum of 1 to 2 days in a normal working week with no restriction on which days these are. What will you be doing?: Projects will include applied evidence syntheses, from evidence maps and rapid reviews to full systematic reviews, and may include methodological work related to evidence synthesis. You will be required to contribute to: The day-to-day review processes and research-related administration of conducting systematic reviews and other evidence syntheses. Developing review protocols, screening studies for inclusion, data collection, risk of bias and GRADE assessments, preparation of datasets, interpretation of data and writing reports. Narrative and quantitative syntheses (e.g. meta-analyses) for a wide range of projects. Peer reviewed publications, grant proposals and conference presentations You should apply if: You have strong training in quantitative research, including knowledge of basic concepts in medical statistics You have an enthusiasm for evidence synthesis and to contribute to living systematic reviews. You have some experience of working on systematic reviews, although this is not a requirement and training will be available You have excellent critical appraisal skills for a range of studies designs For SRA: you have a relevant PhD. For RA: you have a relevant Masters degree (e.g. epidemiology, public health, medical statistics or other relevant quantitative discipline). Additional information: Contract type: Open ended with funding until 31/03/2030 (start date to be agreed, but likely to be 01/11/2026) Work pattern: Full time/ 1 FTE (part-time working will be considered at minimum 0.6 FTE) Grade: I or J/ Pathway 2 Salary: I: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum, J: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum (pro rata if part time) School/Unit: Bristol Medical School Shift pattern: 35 hours per week (part time considered) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 29/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Deborah Caldwell ( Professor of Epidemiology and Public Health: d.m.caldwell@bristol.ac.uk and Julian Higgins ( Professor of Evidence Synthesis, : julian.higgins@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391366": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Animal Behaviour",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108721 The role: We are seeking to appoint a Research Associate to support a BBSRC grant funded under its spotlight call in the area of ‘Fundamental research to enhance animal welfare' and specifically 'measures of welfare, including developing and validating new measures'. Funding is available for 12 months in the first instance with the expectation this will be extended for a further 12 months. The project seeks to develop methods that enable the objective quantification of affective state in different species based on reward learning. We have shown impairments in reward learning have the potential to provide a trans-species, behavioural biomarker of affective state. This project will develop automated methods to quantify reward learning in chickens and dogs as well as expanding our studies in mice. The project will include in lab and field-based behavioural studies and working with engineers to develop novel automated devices. The project includes plans to develop species relevant devices which can be integrated with RFID detection to enable individualised programming and data collection. The ultimate goal will be to validate both the concept of reward learning as a behavioural biomarker of affective state and methods to enable its quantification to facilitate unbiased assessment of animal welfare in different species. What will you be doing?: You will join a team of researchers working across neuroscience, psychopharmacology and animal welfare. The main purpose of the role will be to work with the project engineers and specialist technician to develop and test novel reward delivery devices in different species. This will include input to the design of the devices, evaluating prototypes in mice, chickens and dogs and developing optimised training and testing protocols. We will then run proof of concept studies in the field-testing sensitivity to different affective state. There will also be an opportunity to develop coding expertise both for the development of the behavioural testing protocols and integration with RFID chip readers. You should apply if: You have a passion for animal behaviour and interest in developing novel behavioural technologies. Previous experience in either animal welfare and behaviour, neuroscience and psychology, and/or engineering and computer science would be beneficial, but we recognise this project will involve a range of skills and there will be opportunities to gain additional training where needed. You will need to be comfortable working with a different species and visiting local farms or people’s homes. Good organisational skills are essential as well as an ability to manage your time and multitask. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term (funding available until 30/11/2027) Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 21st October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Emma Robinson, Professor of Psychopharmacology via email: emma.s.j.robinson@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391258": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Dynamic Tissue Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108714 The role: The Weavers Group at the University of Bristol is recruiting a Postdoctoral Research Associate, to join a Wellcome Trust funded programme aimed at uncovering the dynamic cell biology supporting tissue function and repair. Our research seeks to uncover the cellular mechanisms that enable tissues to thrive, particularly under physiological and environmental challenge (see group website https://tissueresilience.com ) . The kidney is a highly complex, physiologically active organ that relies on diverse cell types working together and communicating dynamically. In this position, we will probe how cell biology is precisely patterned and coordinated across this tissue, in homeostasis and following challenge, and why its dysregulation drives ageing and disease. This work builds on recent findings from our team (e.g. Holcombe & Weavers Nature Communications 2023). You will contribute to a multidisciplinary programme combining in vivo studies in Drosophila (with state-of-the-art live-imaging, genetics, molecular cell biology, spatial ‘omics, physiology), with innovative technologies and collaborative works on mammalian tissue and human organ transplantation. Harnessing these powerful approaches, our work spans multiple biological scales - linking the molecular cell biology of individual cells to the health of whole living tissues and organisms. The position offers an exciting opportunity to join a dynamic, enthusiastic team of researchers in Bristol’s Faculty of Health and Life Sciences. Projects can be shaped by the candidate’s interests, with scope for independent directions. The Position is available for three years in the first instance, with potential extension, and offers strong opportunities for collaboration and career development. The position is available from December 2026, although the start date is negotiable. What will you be doing?: You will use Drosophila for unparalleled opportunities to probe the dynamic cell biology of living tissues in vivo. A key focus will be how cellular metabolism and stress responses are coordinated across healthy and damaged tissues, building on our previous work (Holcombe & Weavers Nature Communications 2023; Burbridge et al., Development 2021). You should apply if: You are highly motivated and have a track record commensurate to career stage. You have a PhD in a relevant subject area (or be close to completion). You have prior expertise in cell and/or developmental biology is required, alongside fluency in relevant techniques. Previous experience working with Drosophila models is favoured but not essential. Applications should include a CV and brief statement outlining key areas of expertise and reasons why you would like to join our group. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended, with fixed funding for 3 years Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I Salary: £39,906- £44,746 per annum School/Unit: School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 14th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Helen Weavers, Associate Professor in Cell and Developmental Biology via email: helen.weavers@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391375": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Photocatalytic Enzyme Design",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108720 The role: There is a PDRA post available in the group of Dr Aimee Boyle and Prof. Dek Woolfson FRS at the University of Bristol on a collaborative BBSRC-funded project (with the Universities of Manchester and Edinburgh) to develop de novo designed proteins for photo-catalysis. Your role will be to design proteins with new structures from the bottom-up ( de novo ) to accommodate flavin-like moieties and substrates. You will be responsible for the rational and computational design of the proteins, developing flavination protocols, and producing and characterising the designs structurally using CD and fluorescence spectroscopy and X-ray protein crystallography. The photo-catalytic and enzymatic properties would be characterised in close collaboration with Prof. Nigel Scrutton’s FRS lab in Manchester. Based on the above, you will need to forge good connections and communications with other members of the collaboration to fully realise the designs and evaluate their catalytic activities. Therefore, the appointed postdoc will need a combination of: expertise in the de novo design of proteins, experience in molecular biology, knowledge of protein biophysics and/or structural biology, and good communication skills. This post is available until 31/1/28 in the first instance. What will you be doing?: Specifically, you will design novel coiled-coil-based single-chain proteins assemblies using rationally seeded computational protein design ( Chemical Science 13, 11330-11340, (2022); Nature Chemical Biology 20, 991-999 (2024)). Designs will be produced by recombinant methods in E. coli , and characterised using a variety of techniques with which you must be familiar (e.g. circular dichroism and fluorescence spectroscopy, AUC, crystallography, SAXS, and SEC). You will also explore strategies for incorporating flavin into these scaffolds and the effects that flavin has on the folding and stability of the de novo proteins. Finally, the ability of these scaffolds to act as photocatalysts will be explored in collaboration with our partners at the University of Manchester using fast kinetics and mass spectrometry. You should apply if: The position would be suited to a talented and ambitious early career researcher with expertise in de novo protein design, protein production and characterisation, and an interest in enzymology. As the work will be in collaboration with researchers as both the Universities of Manchester and Edinburgh, it is essential that the successful candidate is keen and able to work as part of a multi-disciplinary team between these groups, and to travel to Manchester and Edinburgh as needed. Additional information: Contract type: OE (Fixed funding until 31/01/2028) Work pattern: 35 hours/week Grade: I Salary: £39,906-£44,746 per annum School/Unit: School of Chemistry Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 14th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Aimee Boyle aimee.boyle@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=341894": {
      "title": "Research Associate in QET LABS /Senior Research Associate",
      "description": "Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD1GA00002 The role: We have a number of exciting research opportunities in the Quantum Engineering Technology Labs ( QET Labs ) at the University of Bristol. As a member of QET Labs, you will conduct research at the interface of quantum technologies, photonics, and experimental science. You will be part of a dynamic team and have the opportunity to interact with all members of QET Labs and the wider Bristol Quantum Information Institute. QET Labs has a mission to take quantum science discoveries out of the lab and engineer them into technologies for the benefit of society. This includes novel routes to quantum computing hardware, quantum communications, enhanced sensing & imaging and new platforms to investigate fundamental quantum physics. The Research Centre consists of over 100 academics, researchers, staff and students and sits within both the School of Physics and the School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering. Multiple Research Associate positions are available of different durations. The duration of the post to be offered will be discussed following a successful interview. What will you be doing?: Your responsibilities will include working with others to formulate project plans, developing new techniques for classical and quantum optics characterisation and delivery of research outputs. Specifically, you will be working in one of these key areas of research: Quantum Computing Quantum Communications Quantum Sensing/Imaging Solid State quantum systems Integrated Quantum Photonics Pure or applied quantum information theory Cold and ultra cold atomic Physics You should apply if: You have a strong track record in the design, implementation, and analysis of photonic devices or quantum information processing with photons You have expertise in optical engineering or quantum optics and a demonstratable understanding of its major platforms, e.g. integrated photonics, emitters You have postgraduate (or equivalent) laboratory experience in one of experimental physics, optics, photonics, electronics, or a related discipline You have a PhD (or equivalent qualification) and first degree in Physics, Electrical/ Electronic Engineering, or other relevant discipline (or equivalent relevant research experience) This position is presented as full-time. Flexible working options are considered. QET Labs is committed to helping researchers become independent academics and have extensive experience in aiding PDRAs with fellowship applications. Please note this is NOT a direct application for a live vacancy. Instead, you will be entered into a candidate pool for a RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN QET LABS /SENIOR RESEARCH ASSOCIATE position. We have a policy in place to regularly review our candidate pool to ensure its relevance and efficiency therefore you may be invited to interview or occasionally receive notifications regarding new job openings. We encourage you to stay connected with us and to keep your contact information up to date to ensure that you don't miss out on any potential opportunities. If you have any questions or need further assistance, please don't hesitate to reach out to us at recruitment@bristol.ac.uk . Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391721": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in Climate Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108738 The role: We are pleased to announce the exciting opportunity of an 18-month Postdoctoral Research Assistant position (Senior Research Associate) at the University of Bristol, UK, in collaboration with the University of Colorado at Boulder, USA, as part of the NSFGEO-NERC funded project \"How well can we constrain past changes in ocean heat and freshwater content?\". The successful candidate will join a US-UK team funded by NSF and NERC, led by the University of Colorado at Boulder, USA (Dr Donata Giglio). The work at the University of Bristol is led by Prof Matt Palmer who also holds the position of Science Fellow at the Met Office Hadley Centre. The research will focus on in situ observations of ocean temperature and the development of ensemble datasets that fully account the uncertainty associated with inter-platform biases. This work will build on research performed at the Met Office Hadley Centre and involve regular contact with scientists working in the Climate Monitoring & Attribution team. The new ensemble datasets will be combined with novel mapping approaches developed by US colleagues at the University of Colorado to provide 3D time-evolving estimates of historical heat and freshwater content. The job holder will also work with scientists at the International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis and the Met Office Hadley Centre to develop and analyse new surface temperature and sea-level rise projections as part of the project. There will be opportunities to visit the US Team in Colorado and attend international conferences and/or working group meetings to present results as part of the project. More details of the project are available from Matt Palmer on request ( m.d.palmer@bristol.ac.uk ). What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will be responsible for developing a new ensemble ocean temperature profile dataset that fully characterises the uncertainty associated with historical inter-platform biases (i.e. differences in apparent ocean temperature that arise through sensor/device characteristics). They will work alongside the US-led team to develop these into ensemble estimates of ocean heat uptake. This improved knowledge of ocean heat uptake and its uncertainty will be used to develop a new set of surface temperature and sea-level rise projections in collaboration with scientists at the Met Office Hadley Centre (UK) and International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (Austria). In addition to the functional aspects of the work, there will be opportunities for research collaboration across many aspects of the project, including mechanistic analysis of ocean heat and freshwater content change and the impact of the new observational constraints on projections of global climate change. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to visit the US-based team at the University of Colorado at Boulder. You should apply if: You will have a PhD or equivalent professional experience in a relevant science discipline. You should also have: Proficiency in the Python programming language Strong quantitative and data analysis skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively And ideally: Experience of modern software development practices, including version control and use of online repositories, such as GitHub Experience in the analysis of geophysical datasets Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding from 01/02/2027 – 31/07/2028 Work pattern: 1 FTE/35 hours per week Grade: I/J Salary: Grade I £33,906 – £44,746 per annum OR Grade J £44,746 - £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Earth Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 16th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Matt Palmer, m.d.palmer@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391718": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in genome-scale metabolic modelling for red blood cell engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108715 The role: Join the School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology at the University of Bristol as a Postdoctoral Research Associate in genome-scale metabolic modelling for red blood cell engineering. This exciting research position forms part of the highly collaborative Aerobic Engineering Biology programme, led by Professor Ashley Toye, which brings together researchers from the University of Bristol, Northwestern University and industrial partners to engineer red blood cells capable of sensing and responding to changes in their metabolic environment. You will lead the metabolic modelling aspects of this programme, developing and applying genome-scale mathematical models to understand red blood cell maturation and predict how engineered biological circuits interact with endogenous cellular metabolism. Working in Professor Lucia Marucci’s group and alongside experimental researchers, you will help create an iterative modelling–experiment cycle that informs the design and optimisation of engineered therapeutic red blood cells. What will you be doing?: You will: Develop and refine genome-scale metabolic models of reticulocytes and mature red blood cells using published knowledge and experimental data generated through the project. Build mathematical models of engineered gene and enzyme circuits and integrate these into broader metabolic modelling frameworks. Analyse metabolomics and other experimental datasets to investigate metabolic changes during red blood cell maturation and engineering. Collaborate closely with experimental researchers at the University of Bristol and Northwestern University to validate models and optimise synthetic circuit design. Work with academic collaborators, industrial partners and the wider research consortium to advance project objectives. Maintain accurate records of code, computational models and research outputs to support reproducible research. Contribute to monthly project progress reports and dissemination of research findings through high-quality publications. You should apply if: You are an enthusiastic researcher with expertise in genome-scale metabolic modelling, mathematical modelling and programming, and you are excited by interdisciplinary research at the interface of engineering biology and computational modelling. You should have: A PhD (or be close to completion) or equivalent research experience in mathematics, bioengineering, engineering biology, bioinformatics, biotechnology or a closely related discipline. Expertise in genome-scale metabolic modelling. Strong mathematical modelling and programming skills. Experience working collaboratively with experimental scientists. Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to publish high-quality research. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a multidisciplinary team and delivering research to agreed deadlines. Experience in engineering biology, multiomics data integration, large collaborative research projects or research funding applications would be advantageous. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding until 05/12/2027 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I or J Salary: £39,906-£44,746 OR £43,482-£50,253 per annum (depending on experience) School/Unit: School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 18th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Lucia Marucci lucia.marucci@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391595": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in Marine Bioacoustics, Conservation, Restoration and Policy",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108712 The role: This role will support the research of Prof. Steve Simpson and his research group as they undertake marine biology research in the UK and overseas. Ongoing projects include: developing soundscape tools to measure biodiversity in the UK, working in partnership with the Cornwall Wildlife Trust and Natural England exploring natural behaviour and impacts of human disturbance in manta rays in the Maldives, in partnership with the Manta Trust building a coral reef restoration programme in the Egyptian Red Sea, working with the Hurghada Environmental Protection and Conservation Association innovating new methods for early life history coral reef restoration, including building an acoustic toolkit, in partnership with Soneva Conservation and Sustainability Maldives and Mars Sustainability Solutions (Indonesia) establishing pathways from scientific innovation to policy and industry, involving organising stakeholder workshops, with collaborations including Mindfully Wired Communication, Sole of Discretion, United Nations Development Programme and Defra You will work closely with Prof. Simpson, supporting a team of research assistants, and PhD and Research Masters students, and build your own research portfolio within these projects. Tasks will include grant-writing, field and admin logistics, data collection and analysis, report and paper writing, conference presentations, stakeholder meetings planning, and public and media outreach. What will you be doing?: You will support postgraduate research students in their desk-based research (e.g. using R for advanced data analysis, modelling, and visualisation), as well as taking a lead on multiple projects through first authoring scientific publications. You will also lead empirical studies, in person and through remote supervision, which are likely to include working in water (SCUBA and snorkelling) and from boats, as well as in lab (e.g. ex situ aquariums) conditions. You will work with group members to prepare scientific publications, reports and briefings. You should apply if: You have a strong interest in one or more of Marine Bioacoustics, Conservation, Restoration and Policy. In this role, you will be supported to lead projects from inception to completion, where organisation, teamwork, leadership and strong communication skills are key. You should have relevant postdoctoral experience, evidence of leading marine-based research teams including postgraduate supervision, a proven track record of securing funding, and a track record of delivering training and stakeholder events. Ideally, you will have boat handling and scuba diving qualifications at a level ready for leading field teams. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding until 31/07/2030 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I or J Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 (Grade I) / £44,746 - £50,253 (Grade J) per annum (depending on experience) School/Unit: School of Biological Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Tuesday 13th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Prof. Steve Simpson, Head of School of Biological Sciences via email: S.Simpson@bristol.ac.uk or tel: +44 7900551883 Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391482": {
      "title": "Research Compliance and Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Department: Research and Enterprise Reference: SUPP113717 The role: This role is designed to specifically support EPSRC-funded Centres for Doctoral Training (CDTs) to comply with research security obligations and contractual arrangements with industry partners. You will be based within the Research Compliance Team with a line into the Research Contracts Team, managed by supervisors in both relevant to the element of work in which you are engaged. This is a groundbreaking role in a sector-leading capacity, joining a dynamic and rapidly evolving team responsible for advising research academics and professional services teams across the University, to ensure research activity is carried out with the highest degree of compliance with relevant legislation. You will be centrally involved in developing relationships with CDT Directors, PhD students and industrial partners, and will have a key role in providing advice and training to research academics and those professional services staff that support them in the conduct of pioneering and prestigious research. Funder focus on compliance with national security regulation and the importance of drafting appropriate collaboration and studentship agreement continues to increase and it will be your responsibility to ensure outcomes that manage internal and external relationships, whilst also protecting the University from the risk of financial, legal and reputation harm. Engagement with internal and external stakeholders, including Government Funders and regulators will be a key element of your role, in addition to developing knowledge-sharing and best practice material in a fast-paced and developing area of law, delivering a critical function in providing specialist advice around Government regulation created to protect UK national security and infrastructure. What will you be doing?: You will: Be involved with investigating and managing enquiries from CDT Directors, supervisors, and postgraduate students specifically pertaining to their research. Assist in establishing policy, processes and guidance around the University's response to legal compliance and contractual measures as relevant to CDTs. Assist with training of professional services teams and research academics in the context of providing advice and awareness around research compliance and contractual requirements from CDTs, and associated risks of non-compliance. Provide input into wider internal communications around prevalent research security risks and in preparing material for updates to SharePoint. Horizon-scanning for arising risks and changes to legislation that could impact the University and, specifically, the CDTs awarded to the institution. Assist in developing a reporting function to showcase practice implementation for each of the 9 CDTs awarded to the University over a 4 year period with the aim of extending its reach and size in the short- and long-term. You should apply if: You are interested in an innovative role combining evolving regulatory compliance with strong commercial and contractual expertise You are interested in developing an expanding area of legal expertise critical to research work in higher education. You are keen to understand new legislation, assess risk, and provide confident and assertive advice to senior personnel with legal and non-legal experience You have an awareness of policy and guidance processes, understanding the importance of audit functions. You have strong problem-solving capability, able to deal with a concurrent workload of different issues with shifting priorities. You have experience of providing supervision to junior staff members, and can assist in mentoring them through legal work experience and education. You have positive interpersonal skills to enable engagement with key stakeholders within the University. You are solutions-focussed, with a proactive and innovative approach to dealing with the challenges of working in a large, research-intensive University. Whilst experience in a regulatory/commercial environment is desirable, it is not essential. Relevant and detailed training will be provided. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until 20/09/2032 Work pattern: Full time Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: Research Compliance This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Wednesday 14th October For informal queries please contact: Chaitali Desai (Head of Research Compliance) on chaitali.desai@bristol.ac.uk or Rajive Sharma ( Senior Research Contracts Manager ) on rajive.sharma@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391684": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Industrial Computer Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108487 The role: The School of Computer Science at the University of Bristol seeks to appoint a Senior Lecturer in Industrial Computer Science to act as academic industry lead within the School. This is a strategic leadership role focused on delivering cutting edge industry education and project skills to our talented students, strengthening our engagement with industrial partners, enhancing graduate employability, developing student and staff entrepreneurship, and aligning education and research with rapidly evolving cutting edge computer science innovations. Working closely with the Head of School and academic and professional colleagues across education, research and impact, you will shape and drive education and partnerships spanning the full range of CS disciplines, formulating and driving forward the School’s industrial strategy. What will you be doing?: Working with professional services colleagues in the School and Faculty, you will deliver pivotal teaching that brings together industry partners and student projects; establish and support the School’s Industrial Advisory Board; lead placement, internship and year-in-industry opportunities; and influence our education to align the curriculum with industry-leading tools and practices. You will promote AI literacy, professional skills development, and international employability, championing student entrepreneurship, staff spinouts and research impact. The role requires coordination within the beyond the School to consolidate employer relationships and enhance students’ transition into a competitive graduate market. You will also have the opportunity to explore entrepreneurial ambitions for yourself and others as part of the School’s participation in the new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus. You should apply if: You have a strong track record in cutting-edge computer science or related fields, alongside substantial experience of industry collaboration, knowledge exchange or entrepreneurship. You will bring credibility with employers, experience of building strategic partnerships, and an understanding of labour market trends in computing. Leadership experience, excellent education and communication skills, and your commitment to student employability and innovation are both important. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: L Salary: £58,225 - £69,488 per annum School/Unit: Computer Science School Team This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 The interviews are anticipated to take place on Friday 20th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Mike Fraser, mike.fraser@bristol.ac.uk , Head of Computer Science Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391618": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Laser Physics for Lithium Isotope Enrichment",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108731 The role: The University of Bristol is seeking a Senior Research Associate to join an ambitious, industrially funded programme focused on the development of advanced laser-based technologies for lithium isotope enrichment. Based within the Interface Analysis Centre, School of Physics, the role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a strategically important research programme that brings together laser physics, spectroscopy, vacuum engineering and process development. Working within a multidisciplinary team led by Professor Tom Scott, Dr Jacek Wasik and Dr Dave Megson-Smith, you will provide specialist expertise in laser physics and optical systems while helping to establish a new experimental capability. The position offers a unique opportunity to work at the interface between academic research and industrial innovation, contributing to the development of novel technologies with the potential for significant scientific and commercial impact. What will you be doing?: You will lead the development, commissioning and optimisation of advanced laser and optical systems that underpin the isotope enrichment process. This will include designing and integrating laser sources, spectroscopy tools and optical diagnostics, while ensuring the performance, stability and precision required for experimental success. A major focus of the role will be planning and conducting spectroscopy and process-development experiments to investigate isotope-selective interactions and evaluate process performance. You will analyse data, interpret results and apply scientific insight to solve complex technical challenges, helping to guide future research activities. Working closely with engineers, physicists and technical specialists, you will integrate laser systems with vacuum equipment, diagnostics, control systems and wider facility infrastructure. You will also contribute to project planning, equipment procurement, commissioning and technical reporting, taking responsibility for delivering key milestones and maintaining high standards of safety, quality and reproducibility. As part of an industrially funded programme, you will work closely with private-sector partners and suppliers, contributing to technical reviews and ensuring research outputs are delivered to professional and commercial standards. Success in the role requires the ability to operate effectively within a collaborative environment while balancing scientific excellence, project delivery, confidentiality and stakeholder expectations. You should apply if: You have a PhD, or equivalent research experience, in physics, optical engineering or a related discipline, together with significant hands-on experience in laser physics, optics and spectroscopy. You enjoy solving challenging experimental problems and have experience designing, commissioning and troubleshooting complex laser systems. You will be highly organised, collaborative and capable of working independently while delivering results in a fast-paced research environment. Strong communication skills and the ability to build productive relationships with industrial partners are essential, as is a commitment to laboratory safety, research quality and technical excellence. This role would suit someone excited by the opportunity to help develop a unique UK capability in laser-enabled isotope science. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term for up to 11 months Work pattern: 1 FTE Grade: J Salary: £44,746 to £50,253 per annum School/Unit: School of Physics Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 21/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Jacek Wasik, jacek.wasik@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391679": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Protein Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108737 The role: We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join Professor Ashley Toye’s group in the School of Biochemistry at the University of Bristol. This full-time position (35 hours per week) is offered on a six-month fixed-term contract funded by the Defence Science and Technology Laboratory (Dstl). The Toye group studies red blood cell biology and develops approaches that use red blood cells as platforms for biologically active proteins. You will contribute to a collaborative feasibility study at the interface of protein engineering, biochemistry and red blood cell biology. What will you be doing?: You will design, produce and characterise recombinant proteins and investigate their interactions with red blood cells. The work will involve molecular cloning, bacterial protein expression, affinity and size exclusion chromatography, and biochemical activity assays. You will assess cell binding and retention using flow cytometry and microfluidic flow systems, with mammalian protein expression where required. You will plan and troubleshoot experiments, analyse quantitative data, maintain accurate records and contribute to project meetings, progress updates and the final report. You should apply if: You hold a PhD in biochemistry, molecular biology, protein engineering, synthetic biology or a related discipline, or have submitted your thesis with the award expected before the appointment begins. You have practical experience of recombinant DNA methods, bacterial protein expression and protein purification, and can design experiments and interpret quantitative results. You can organise your work to meet deadlines and communicate clearly within a collaborative team. Experience of red blood cell biology, flow cytometry, mammalian expression, enzyme assays, microfluidics or computational protein design would be an advantage. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed-term funding until 31/03/2027 Work pattern: Full-time (100% time) Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 p.a School/Unit: School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 25th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Prof Ashley Toye (ash.m.toye@bristol.ac.uk) Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391694": {
      "title": "Specialist Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113771 The role: The School of Chemistry is seeking a highly motivated and skilled Specialist Research Technician to provide expert technical support across a diverse portfolio of research activities within Physical Chemistry and shared research facilities. This is an exciting opportunity to support internationally recognised research by working closely with academic staff, researchers and students across a range of disciplines including atmospheric chemistry, aerosol science, electrochemistry, colloid and formulation science, mass spectrometry, laser chemistry and materials research. The successful candidate will play a key role in the operation, maintenance and development of specialist laboratory facilities and advanced analytical instrumentation. You will provide technical expertise to support research projects, manage laboratory equipment and infrastructure, develop safe working practices and train researchers in the effective use of specialist facilities. This role is ideally suited to someone who enjoys solving technical challenges, working with complex scientific equipment and supporting cutting-edge research in a collaborative academic environment. Please note due to the nature of the role we require the role holder to be based primarily on campus What will you be doing?: Supporting research staff and students through the operation, maintenance and troubleshooting of specialist analytical instrumentation. Managing and maintaining laboratory facilities to ensure equipment is safe, reliable and available for research use. Installing, commissioning and servicing scientific equipment and associated laboratory infrastructure. Training and advising researchers on the safe and effective use of specialist instrumentation. Working closely with academic staff to develop technical solutions that support research projects. Ensuring compliance with health and safety requirements through the development of safe working practices and documentation. You should apply if: You have experience working with advanced scientific instrumentation in a research, technical or 3 years plus laboratory environment. You have strong practical problem-solving skills and enjoy diagnosing and resolving technical issues. You have experience supporting laboratory users and delivering technical training. You can manage a varied workload and work effectively with multiple stakeholders. You have a commitment to safe working practices and high-quality service delivery. You enjoy working collaboratively and want to contribute to world-leading research. Desirable: Experience with chromatography, mass spectrometry or other analytical instrumentation. Experience with gas handling systems, vacuum systems or laboratory infrastructure. Experience supporting research laboratories or shared scientific facilities. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term for 12 months (secondment cover) Work pattern: Full time Grade: G Salary: £32,080 - £35,608 per annum School/Unit: School of Chemistry This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 25/10/26 For informal queries please contact: Charlotte Edom-Hodge, Head of Operations Email : charlotte.edom-hodge@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391561": {
      "title": "Talent Academy Coach",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113812 The role: The University of Bristol is a top 10 university for sport. The Sport, Exercise & Health Department works with more than 70 student sport clubs, encouraging participation, enhancing the student experience & pursuing athletic performance, whilst supporting Academic excellence. The Sport Exercise & Health Department (SEH) also fosters strong community links, partnering with local schools, clubs & national governing bodies to deliver programmes from participation through to performance for the Bristol community. The #Wearebristol hashtag demonstrates our ambition beyond university life, we want to be an organisation that can represent Bristol, that the city can be proud of. Leveraging the university performance environment, utilising high quality coaching & athlete development as a proud ‘independent’ organisation, the University of Bristol Talent Academy seeks to develop & support the widest range of young people in hockey in the Bristol area. The Hockey Talent Academy, an England Hockey licenced Programme began life in September 2023 and is now in it’s second year of operation, following a highly successful first year bringing together 55-60 young people from across the region for in the order of 280 hours of activity, on-pitch training, strength & conditioning training, development & competitive matches, off pitch game education & video review, sports psychology & nutrition classes. The SEH Performance Team seek passionate, creative & highly skilled coaches to join our team. Building on established foundations, embracing the culture of excellence & people-focused development that exists today within both the Hockey Club & Talent Academy, the successful candidate will playing a key role in the continuation and enhancement of an inspiring, positive & supportive development focused performance culture at the hockey Talent Academy, mirroring that of the wider University of Bristol hockey & performance culture. What will you be doing?: The post-holder will support delivery of ‘day-to-day’ coaching for one of the Boys / Girls groups of c.25 athletes within the 4 hours per week. You will deliver quality individual & team focused coaching according to the England Hockey Talent System Framework & in line with planning developed by the University of Bristol Head of Hockey, Talent Academy Lead Coach &/or Talent Academy Group lead. The successful candidate will Deliver high-quality high intensity, person-centred coaching suitable for young athletes. Sessions to be game-based, representative learning, utilising other elements of the session design toolkit as appropriate. Assist / coach / umpire matches in support of training groups. Utilise Individual Development Plans to shape targeted coaching interventions. Use video & other tools as part of this. Provide detailed, developmental feedback to athletes that reflects both individuals learning objectives & observed performance. Support formal written feedback processes as well as informal feedback integrated within sessions. Contribute to fair and open, person focused selection processes, supporting all athletes with the same opportunities and an individual approach. Lead, work alongside lead coach & the wider coaching team, embodying a culture of continuous improvement, development & teamwork. Be part of planning meetings including for training planning, squad selection & communicate regularly with the Lead Coach / TA Head Coach. Communicate with participants & key stakeholders within their networks in an open, supportive, positive, collaborative manner, You should apply if: The successful candidate should Bring extensive experience of developing high potential (young) players in club & performance environments & ideally significant experience working within the England Hockey Talent System or former player Pathway. Demonstrate a track record of connecting with young people, parents & other coaches and as part of athlete personal development & wellbeing. Have active accredited coach status with England Hockey, holding at minimum England Hockey Level 2 Coach Award, alongside an ability to demonstrate continuous personal development beyond this. Hold an up to date Safeguarding Certificate. Demonstrate experience of team-working / co-coaching with other coaches to deliver a safe, prosperous & high quality development environment. Be an excellent, rounded leader of people, focusing on developing a long-term culture of learning & performance. Be passionate about sport as part of a rounded lifestyle. Be conscientious, committed, punctual & reliable. Demonstrate excellent verbal & written communication skills. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time, 4 hours Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Centre for Sport Exercise & Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 18/10/2026 The University SEH Performance team is more than happy to discuss the role informally prior to the application deadline. Please contact Martin Hicks ( martin.hicks@bristol.ac.uk ) with any questions. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391605": {
      "title": "University TSS - Admin and Clerical pool",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Department: Human Resources Reference: Tss100011 The role: The Temporary Staffing Service (TSS) provides temporary administrative and clerical work opportunities across the University of Bristol. We have a variety of full time and part time assignments covering a wide range of skills in a variety of Faculties, Schools and Divisions. Joining the TSS will give you a chance to join an organisation with an exciting and thriving work environment, allowing you to develop your skills and experience. What will you be doing?: You will need to apply to register with the TSS and if successful you will join our pool. This means that as assignments come up that match your skills, experience and indicated availability we will let you know to see whether you’re interested. For more information about the TSS please see our website: Admin and clerical | Temporary Staffing Service | University of Bristol You should apply if: You have strong admin and clerical skills, and you would like the flexibility and variety that temporary work can offer. You are able to provide a clear and concise CV, preferably no more than 2 pages, clearly demonstrating your administrative skills and experience. You are available to start working immediately. We are currently only looking for applicants with full time availability."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391623": {
      "title": "Widening Participation Recruitment Officer (Race and Ethnicity)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113753 The role: The role will be a key member of the Widening Participation team, helping to ensure the University effectively delivers on our Access commitments as outlined in our Access and Participation Plan (APP). The Widening Participation team’s purpose is to increase access to the University for students from disadvantaged, minoritised or underrepresented backgrounds, engaging with social and structural barriers and supporting the diversification of our student community. This is one of a team of Officers who deliver multiple projects, partnerships and initiatives to support a broad range of students and to increase applications and intake to the University. The role is varied and fast paced and involves working with a range of stakeholders across the team, the wider University, and in local and national schools and colleges. The role is ideal for someone with an agile and responsive mindset who is looking for a challenge within a supportive team and who is interested in working in strategically important areas of recruitment and Widening Participation (WP). What will you be doing?: You will lead on a range of widening participation projects and initiatives, supporting and recruiting post-16 students from underrepresented and disadvantaged backgrounds, with a particular focus on recruiting Black and Asian students to the University. Your remit may include leading an admissions-benefit project such as Next Step Bristol; leading our APP Student Advisory Group; coordinating communications as part of our Offer Holder Horizons email campaign; and supporting other projects such as The Destinations Conference and Insight Into Bristol. You will also work with target schools and colleges, with an emphasis on engaging students from underrepresented groups such as Global Majority audiences and students eligible for Free School Meals. Working collaboratively with a team of Widening Participation Recruitment Officers and Mature Student Recruitment Officers, you will monitor application and intake statistics and interpret this data to develop appropriate Widening Participation activities. There are also opportunities to gain experience in a range of strategic settings, including admissions processes and procedures, designing and delivering recruitment activity, data analysis, partnership development and relationship management. As we continue to deliver the University’s 2025/26 - 2028/29 APP, our WP strategy will evolve and different areas of focus may emerge requiring the postholder to work across different remits in a collaborative environment. You should apply if: You are experienced working with marginalised, underrepresented or disadvantaged groups, particularly Global Majority heritage backgrounds, and are committed to driving equalising change for students from these groups You enjoy working in collaborative teams, working across a diverse portfolio of projects You are organised and details-orientated and have excellent time management skills, with the ability to be flexible and balance multiple work streams, adjusting to changing priorities You have excellent data and evaluation skills, with experience of designing and implementing evaluation frameworks, and enjoy analysing complex datasets to find innovative solutions You are resilient and positive, even when dealing with complex and challenging problems You are confident and assertive and can communicate well with anyone with your excellent relationship building skills You can deliver engaging, effective activities and events for large cohorts, both in person and online You have a keen interest in changing the University for the better and making it more accessible for underrepresented groups in the long term, as well as an ability to identify and action “quick wins” to make a difference to students in the short term Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time, 35 hours per week Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: Education and Student Success This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 27th October For informal queries please contact Sarah Almond, sarah.almond@bristol.ac.uk or Sarah-Jane Steele, sarah-jane.kinley@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    }
  },
